1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,055
A join is how you read two tables as if they were one honest list.

2
00:00:05,055 --> 00:00:06,819
customers has names.

3
00:00:06,819 --> 00:00:08,361
orders has totals.

4
00:00:08,361 --> 00:00:12,500
Neither table has the full sentence: Maya spent forty-two.

5
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:16,138
The sentence appears when customer_id matches.

6
00:00:16,138 --> 00:00:23,777
FROM customers JOIN orders ON customers.customer_id equals orders.customer_id.

7
00:00:23,777 --> 00:00:25,319
ON is the match rule.

8
00:00:25,319 --> 00:00:29,083
It is not a filter in the WHERE sense, though it looks like one.

9
00:00:29,083 --> 00:00:33,083
It says which row on the left belongs with which row on the right.

10
00:00:33,083 --> 00:00:36,888
Inner join keeps a pair only when both sides match.

11
00:00:36,888 --> 00:00:40,736
Maya has two orders, so Maya appears twice.

12
00:00:40,736 --> 00:00:42,833
That duplication is correct.

13
00:00:42,833 --> 00:00:44,916
She really did order twice.

14
00:00:44,916 --> 00:00:48,569
A customer with no orders disappears from an inner join.

15
00:00:48,569 --> 00:00:52,930
An order whose customer_id is missing disappears too.

16
00:00:52,930 --> 00:00:56,291
Left join keeps every row from the left table.

17
00:00:56,291 --> 00:00:59,736
When there is no match, the right-hand columns are NULL.

18
00:00:59,736 --> 00:01:02,541
Those empty cells are the point, not a bug.

19
00:01:02,541 --> 00:01:05,236
They show you customers who have not ordered yet.

20
00:01:05,236 --> 00:01:08,513
Right join keeps every row from the right table.

21
00:01:08,513 --> 00:01:12,750
Most people write a left join and swap the table order instead.

22
00:01:12,750 --> 00:01:16,444
Full join keeps unmatched rows from both sides.

23
00:01:16,444 --> 00:01:19,833
SQLite does not ship a full join keyword.

24
00:01:19,833 --> 00:01:24,111
You can still build the idea with a union of two left joins.

25
00:01:24,111 --> 00:01:27,555
CROSS JOIN pairs every row with every row.

26
00:01:27,555 --> 00:01:31,138
Four customers and four orders become sixteen rows.

27
00:01:31,138 --> 00:01:34,722
Use it only when you mean a combination, not a match.

28
00:01:34,722 --> 00:01:39,305
When a result explodes in size, you probably forgot the ON clause.

29
00:01:39,305 --> 00:01:42,722
That accidental cross join is a famous mess.

30
00:01:42,722 --> 00:01:45,972
Natural join matches columns that share a name.

31
00:01:45,972 --> 00:01:48,486
It saves typing and hides the rule.

32
00:01:48,486 --> 00:01:50,958
Prefer ON with explicit columns.

33
00:01:50,958 --> 00:01:52,555
You can see the contract.

34
00:01:52,555 --> 00:01:55,777
Join more than two tables by repeating the pattern.

35
00:01:55,777 --> 00:01:59,444
Match orders to customers, then orders to stores.

36
00:01:59,444 --> 00:02:01,500
Each ON is one relationship.

37
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:04,694
WHERE after a join filters the paired rows.

38
00:02:04,694 --> 00:02:09,041
Put city equals Austin in WHERE if you only want Austin pairs.

39
00:02:09,041 --> 00:02:12,611
Do not put the match itself in WHERE if you meant a left join.

40
00:02:12,611 --> 00:02:18,083
A condition on the right table in WHERE can turn a left join back into an inner join.

41
00:02:18,083 --> 00:02:19,694
Keep match rules in ON.

42
00:02:19,694 --> 00:02:23,111
Keep extra filters in WHERE, and watch NULLs.

43
00:02:23,111 --> 00:02:25,263
Aliases keep the sentence short.

44
00:02:25,263 --> 00:02:33,513
FROM customers AS c JOIN orders AS o ON c.customer_id equals o.customer_id.

45
00:02:33,513 --> 00:02:36,638
Qualify every column when names collide.

46
00:02:36,638 --> 00:02:41,361
Run the inner join, then the left join, on the same ON clause.

47
00:02:41,361 --> 00:02:43,041
Compare the row counts.

48
00:02:43,041 --> 00:02:46,236
The extra empty cells tell you who has no partner.

49
00:02:46,236 --> 00:02:48,333
That is the join doing its job.

50
00:02:48,333 --> 00:02:51,500
A join condition with OR is usually a mistake.

51
00:02:51,500 --> 00:02:55,166
You meant two different relationships, or you meant a filter.

52
00:02:55,166 --> 00:02:56,388
Keep ON tight.

53
00:02:56,388 --> 00:03:02,458
Inequality joins exist: every order whose total is greater than a threshold row.

54
00:03:02,458 --> 00:03:04,347
They are rare in this course.

55
00:03:04,347 --> 00:03:06,694
They can explode like a cross join.

56
00:03:06,694 --> 00:03:08,930
Prefer equality on keys.

57
00:03:08,930 --> 00:03:12,555
USING is a shorthand when both columns share a name.

58
00:03:12,555 --> 00:03:15,486
JOIN orders USING customer_id.

59
00:03:15,486 --> 00:03:20,291
It also folds the two columns into one in the result in some engines.

60
00:03:20,291 --> 00:03:22,180
ON is clearer while you learn.

61
00:03:22,180 --> 00:03:23,972
Select the columns you need.

62
00:03:23,972 --> 00:03:30,222
SELECT star after a join duplicates customer_id and hides which table it came from.

63
00:03:30,222 --> 00:03:31,250
Qualify.

64
00:03:31,250 --> 00:03:36,722
c.customer_id, o.order_id, o.total.

65
00:03:36,722 --> 00:03:40,166
When you join three tables, walk with your finger.

66
00:03:40,166 --> 00:03:43,361
customers to orders on customer_id.

67
00:03:43,361 --> 00:03:46,291
orders to lines on order_id.

68
00:03:46,291 --> 00:03:50,541
If a hop is missing, you invented a cross join in the middle.

69
00:03:50,541 --> 00:03:56,402
COUNT after a join counts pairs, not customers, unless you DISTINCT or group.

70
00:03:56,402 --> 00:03:59,777
Maya's two orders become two in the count.

71
00:03:59,777 --> 00:04:03,000
That is why summaries often group after they join.

72
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:05,944
NULL on the match column never equals NULL.

73
00:04:05,944 --> 00:04:09,888
Those rows only survive a left join as unmatched.

74
00:04:09,888 --> 00:04:17,361
If you stored missing customers as zero, zero will join to nothing useful unless you have a fake customer zero.

75
00:04:17,361 --> 00:04:22,847
Prefer NULL and a left join, or a real guest customer row, and pick one.

76
00:04:22,847 --> 00:04:29,944
Practice with the four orders and three customers until the inner and left counts are numbers you can explain.

77
00:04:29,944 --> 00:04:32,388
Then the syntax is just spelling.

78
00:04:32,388 --> 00:04:34,319
The idea is the match.

79
00:04:34,319 --> 00:04:35,583
Name the match.

80
00:04:35,583 --> 00:04:36,666
Keep it in ON.

81
00:04:36,666 --> 00:04:40,000
Then choose which unmatched rows you still want to see.

82
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:44,555
A join is how you ask a question that no single table can answer.

83
00:04:44,555 --> 00:04:46,833
Product names live on products.

84
00:04:46,833 --> 00:04:49,208
Review text lives on reviews.

85
00:04:49,322 --> 00:04:53,202
The question which reviews belong to which product needs both.

86
00:04:53,202 --> 00:05:01,716
FROM products JOIN reviews ON products.product_id equals reviews.product_id is the map.

87
00:05:01,716 --> 00:05:03,369
ON is the matching rule.

88
00:05:03,369 --> 00:05:04,966
It is not decoration.

89
00:05:04,966 --> 00:05:09,452
Without a matching rule, every product pairs with every review.

90
00:05:09,452 --> 00:05:14,160
That Cartesian product is huge, and almost every pair is a lie.

91
00:05:14,160 --> 00:05:16,202
The engine is not being clever.

92
00:05:16,202 --> 00:05:19,994
It is being literal: you asked for every combination.

93
00:05:19,994 --> 00:05:22,994
INNER JOIN keeps only pairs that match.

94
00:05:22,994 --> 00:05:26,605
A product with no reviews disappears from an inner join.

95
00:05:26,605 --> 00:05:32,160
A review whose product_id is missing from products also disappears.

96
00:05:32,160 --> 00:05:35,535
LEFT JOIN keeps every row from the left table.

97
00:05:35,535 --> 00:05:38,980
When there is no match, the right-hand columns are NULL.

98
00:05:38,980 --> 00:05:46,535
That is how you find products that have never been reviewed: LEFT JOIN, then WHERE review_id IS NULL.

99
00:05:46,535 --> 00:05:49,855
RIGHT JOIN keeps every row from the right table.

100
00:05:49,855 --> 00:05:57,313
FULL OUTER JOIN, where the engine has it, keeps rows from both sides, with NULLs where a match is missing.

101
00:05:57,313 --> 00:06:00,341
Most day-to-day work is inner or left.

102
00:06:00,341 --> 00:06:04,355
Say which rows you refuse to lose before you pick the join type.

103
00:06:04,355 --> 00:06:07,702
WHERE after a join filters the combined rows.

104
00:06:07,702 --> 00:06:14,035
Putting a right-table filter in WHERE on a left join can accidentally turn it into an inner join.

105
00:06:14,035 --> 00:06:20,785
If you meant to keep unmatched left rows, put that filter in ON, or test IS NULL on purpose.

106
00:06:20,785 --> 00:06:22,897
JOIN is about how rows meet.

107
00:06:22,897 --> 00:06:25,938
WHERE is about which combined rows you still want.

108
00:06:25,938 --> 00:06:33,244
Aliases shorten table names so products.product_id can be p.product_id.

109
00:06:33,244 --> 00:06:37,244
Once you alias, use the alias everywhere in that query.

110
00:06:37,244 --> 00:06:41,230
Qualify every column that exists on more than one table.

111
00:06:41,230 --> 00:06:46,938
product_id without a table name is ambiguous once both tables have it.

112
00:06:46,938 --> 00:06:50,549
You can join more than two tables, one pair at a time.

113
00:06:50,549 --> 00:06:55,855
Each ON should name a real key relationship, not a guess that two names look similar.

114
00:06:55,855 --> 00:07:01,160
Joining on city because both tables have a city column is usually a mistake.

115
00:07:01,160 --> 00:07:03,230
Cities are not identifiers.

116
00:07:03,230 --> 00:07:04,202
Keys are.

117
00:07:04,202 --> 00:07:12,160
Read a join query as: start from this table, bring matching rows from that table, then keep the ones that still matter.

118
00:07:12,160 --> 00:07:17,244
If the row count explodes, you probably matched too loosely or forgot ON.

119
00:07:17,244 --> 00:07:22,869
If the row count collapses to nothing, you probably inner-joined past a missing parent.

120
00:07:22,869 --> 00:07:26,438
Check counts at each join when the result looks wrong.

121
00:07:26,438 --> 00:07:28,202
The join is not magic.

122
00:07:28,202 --> 00:07:31,535
It is a matching rule you can say in one sentence.

123
00:07:31,535 --> 00:07:35,202
If you cannot say the sentence, do not write the JOIN yet.

124
00:07:35,202 --> 00:07:38,452
WHERE versus JOIN, as the lecture insists.

125
00:07:38,452 --> 00:07:48,313
Old style FROM products, reviews WHERE products.product_id equals reviews.product_id is an inner join in costume.

126
00:07:48,313 --> 00:07:51,716
Miss the WHERE and you have a Cartesian product again.

127
00:07:51,716 --> 00:07:54,452
JOIN ON makes the matching rule visible.

128
00:07:54,452 --> 00:07:56,355
Prefer the visible form.

129
00:07:56,355 --> 00:08:03,452
A WHERE that further filters price greater than twenty after the join is a row filter on the combined result.

130
00:08:03,452 --> 00:08:05,105
It is not a second join.

131
00:08:05,105 --> 00:08:08,924
People confuse them because both can mention two tables.

132
00:08:08,924 --> 00:08:10,619
ON is for how they meet.

133
00:08:10,619 --> 00:08:12,563
WHERE is for who remains.

134
00:08:12,563 --> 00:08:18,549
If you write LEFT JOIN and then WHERE right.key IS NOT NULL, you asked for inner.

135
00:08:18,549 --> 00:08:20,799
Write INNER JOIN if that is what you meant.

136
00:08:20,799 --> 00:08:33,008
The comparison of WHERE and JOIN is this: one combines, one selects, and stuffing combine logic into WHERE is how 2010 tutorials hid the Cartesian risk.

137
00:08:33,008 --> 00:08:34,174
Do not hide it.

138
00:08:34,174 --> 00:08:37,438
JOIN ON, then WHERE, then the rest.

139
00:08:37,438 --> 00:08:41,077
Close with questions, the way a careful lecture does.

140
00:08:41,077 --> 00:08:44,133
Answer in a sentence you could defend at a keyboard.

141
00:08:44,133 --> 00:08:45,827
What question needs a join?

142
00:08:45,827 --> 00:08:48,313
One that no single table can answer.

143
00:08:48,313 --> 00:08:49,466
What does ON do?

144
00:08:49,466 --> 00:08:52,508
It states the matching rule, usually a key.

145
00:08:52,508 --> 00:08:54,591
What is a Cartesian product here?

146
00:08:54,591 --> 00:08:58,049
Every row paired with every row because ON was missing.

147
00:08:58,049 --> 00:08:59,674
What does INNER JOIN drop?

148
00:08:59,674 --> 00:09:02,147
Unmatched rows from both sides.

149
00:09:02,147 --> 00:09:03,799
What does LEFT JOIN keep?

150
00:09:03,799 --> 00:09:07,855
Every row from the left table, NULL on the right when missing.

151
00:09:07,855 --> 00:09:10,202
How do you find parents with no children?

152
00:09:10,202 --> 00:09:13,674
LEFT JOIN, then WHERE child key IS NULL.

153
00:09:13,674 --> 00:09:17,216
When does WHERE turn a left join into an inner join?

154
00:09:17,216 --> 00:09:19,855
When it demands a non-NULL right column.

155
00:09:19,855 --> 00:09:21,272
Join versus WHERE?

156
00:09:21,272 --> 00:09:24,813
JOIN combines, WHERE filters the combination.

157
00:09:24,813 --> 00:09:26,466
Why alias tables?

158
00:09:26,466 --> 00:09:32,022
Shorter names, required once you alias, and to qualify shared column names.

159
00:09:32,138 --> 00:09:34,685
Why qualify product_id?

160
00:09:34,685 --> 00:09:36,907
Both tables have it after a join.

161
00:09:36,907 --> 00:09:39,213
Is joining on city usually right?

162
00:09:39,213 --> 00:09:39,976
No.

163
00:09:39,976 --> 00:09:42,046
Cities are not identifiers.

164
00:09:42,046 --> 00:09:44,074
Why did the product name repeat?

165
00:09:44,074 --> 00:09:48,851
Two reviews, so two result rows; the grain is the review.

166
00:09:48,851 --> 00:09:50,879
Should you SUM price on that grain?

167
00:09:50,879 --> 00:09:53,949
Not if you would count the product twice by accident.

168
00:09:53,949 --> 00:09:56,115
Old comma FROM with WHERE match?

169
00:09:56,115 --> 00:09:59,838
An inner join in costume, easy to forget the match.

170
00:09:59,838 --> 00:10:01,949
What do you count after each join?

171
00:10:01,949 --> 00:10:05,643
Row counts, to catch explosion or collapse.

172
00:10:05,643 --> 00:10:07,851
RIGHT JOIN in one sentence?

173
00:10:07,851 --> 00:10:10,296
Keep every row from the right table.

174
00:10:10,296 --> 00:10:12,435
FULL OUTER in one sentence?

175
00:10:12,435 --> 00:10:16,449
Keep rows from both sides, NULL where a match is missing.

176
00:10:16,449 --> 00:10:18,560
Which two types do most work?

177
00:10:18,560 --> 00:10:19,990
INNER and LEFT.

178
00:10:19,990 --> 00:10:22,546
What sentence must you say before JOIN?

179
00:10:22,546 --> 00:10:24,032
How these rows meet.

180
00:10:24,032 --> 00:10:25,435
If you cannot say it?

181
00:10:25,435 --> 00:10:27,213
Do not write the JOIN yet.

182
00:10:27,213 --> 00:10:31,935
If an answer needed a second table, go draw it before you write more SQL.

